\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The change to scalar frames and its counting factor}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For an \vocabk{orbit section}{fdip-010X}, retaining the scalar
field and replacing every link by its reduced link gives a bijection
from joint configurations to #{\mathcal O^X\times G^E}.
In these variables the joint weight depends only on the reduced
connection. For a link tuple #{V\in G^E} its value is
##{\check\Xi(V)=\prod_f a_f(V_f)\,
  \prod_e b_e(\phi^\circ,V_e\phi^\circ)\,
  \prod_x c_x(\phi^\circ),}
where #{V_f} is its based face transport. With unnormalized
counting over the commutative unital ring #{k},
##{\check Z=\sum_{V\in G^E}\check\Xi(V),\qquad
 Z=|\mathcal O|^{|X|}\check Z.}
For a free orbit the count factor is #{|G|^{|X|}}, and each
reduced link itself is invariant under vertex gauges.}

\proof{
\p{The inverse uses the retained field to recover its frames, then
sets #{U_e=g_{h(e)}V_e g_{s(e)}^{-1}}. The gauge with value
#{g_x^{-1}} at each site takes the original configuration to #{V}
with constant scalar #{\phi^\circ}. By \ref{fdip-010C} its weight
is unchanged. The face holonomy changes by conjugation at the base,
also for words using reverse edges. Substituting the constant
scalar gives the displayed product. Each of the
#{|\mathcal O|^{|X|}} scalar configurations contributes the same
sum over #{V}, giving the identity without division.}

\p{For a free orbit the orbit map #{g\mapsto g\phi^\circ} is a
bijection. After a vertex gauge #{a}, the unique frame at #{x}
is #{a_xg_x}. Substituting this and the transformed link cancels
both endpoint gauges, leaving #{\check U_e} unchanged.}
}
